course: dictionary
lesson: 9692
title: Words from history
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words tell a story about the past. «The British suffragettes, led by Emmeline Pankhurst, used bolder actions such as chaining themselves to railings.» Chaining yourself to railings is a brave, noisy protest, so a suffragette must be a woman who campaigned loudly for something.
explain: «Suffragists campaigned peacefully with petitions and marches, while the suffragettes in Britain chose bolder deeds.» The word «while» shows a contrast. Suffragists were peaceful, so the suffragettes must be the bolder group. Both groups wanted the same thing: votes for women.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The suffragettes wanted women to have the right to vote» still makes sense, so you have understood it. If a word is still unclear, check a dictionary.
fact: A suffragette was a woman, especially in Britain about a hundred years ago, who campaigned for women to have the right to vote.
fact: «Suffrage» means the right to vote.
fact: A suffragist also fought for women's votes, but usually in peaceful ways such as petitions and marches.
fact: New Zealand was the first country where women could vote in national elections.
fact: The suffragettes' motto was «Deeds not words», and their colours were purple, white and green.
exercise: «Suffragists campaigned peacefully with petitions and marches, while the suffragettes in Britain chose bolder deeds.» Which group chose bolder deeds? | The suffragists | *The suffragettes | The petitions | The marches
exercise: Your great-great-gran may not have been allowed to vote because she was a woman. What does «suffrage» mean? | A colour of a flag | A kind of prison | *The right to vote | The right to march in the streets
exercise: What did suffragettes campaign for? | Men to be sent to prison | Bigger windows in schools | *Women to have the right to vote | Women to run every shop in town
test: Which country was the first where women could vote in national elections? | Britain | Australia | France | *New Zealand
test: What were the suffragettes' colours? | Pink and silver | *Purple, white and green | Black and orange | Red, blue and gold
exam: What is a suffragette? | A man who ran a petrol station | A queen who ruled Britain for a hundred years | *A woman who campaigned, often with bold protests, for women to have the right to vote | A teacher who taught history in a school
